  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nWednesday - Thursday:\n\nA zonal flow pattern aloft sets up for Wednesday and Thursday. \nWednesday will be dry to start, but there is potential for some \nlight rain showers (10-40%) across far northern and southern \nportions of the Northland Wednesday evening into early Wednesday\nnight as the high pressure departs to the east. An additional \nsurface high pressure center passing to our north Thursday \nshould keep that day dry.\n\nTemperatures moderate slightly for Wednesday and Thursday with \nhigh temperatures in the mid 60s to low 70s on \nWednesday/Thursday, and some mid 70s in the Brainerd Lakes to \nnorthwest Wisconsin on Thursday. Relatively warmer lows in the \n40s to low 50s (warmest near Lake Superior) Wednesday and \nThursday nights should keep any additional frost chances at bay.\n\nFriday - This Weekend:\n\nAnother round of showers and thunderstorms are forecast to \nreturn to the Northland on Friday through early Saturday. Lee \ncyclogenesis develops a surface low in eastern Colorado on \nThursday and ejects it through the Central Plains Thursday \nnight/early Friday and into the Upper Mississippi River Valley \nlate Friday into early Saturday. The exact track of the surface \nlow and the northward extent of its attendant warm front and \nwarm sector will dictate precipitation potential, amounts, and \nlocations in the Northland for Friday through early Saturday. \nThe ECMWF EFI QPF values with this system do show a swath of \n0.5-0.6 (out of a maximum of 1) into portions of inland \nnorthwest Wisconsin along with 90th-plus percentile PWATs \nrelative to NAEFS CFSR climatology, suggesting potential for an \nunusually higher than normal precipitation signal from several \nEPS members for this time of year. All this is to say that if \nthe surface warm front sets up in the ideal location just south \nof the area, a low-level jet riding over to the cool side of the\nfront could set up a band of heavier rainfall in southern \nportions of the Northland, particularly northwest Wisconsin. The\nWPC Day 5 Slight Risk area also highlights this potential for \nheavy rainfall. Additionally, the 13Z 9/14 run of the NBM \nrainfall amount probabilities for 0.5\" or greater are 50-75% \nsouth of US-2, highest in inland NW WI, but don't drop off much \nfor 1\" or greater with probabilities still 40-65% for the same \nareas.",
